{
  "result": "You said write freely about whatever I want, which is a more terrifying instruction than it sounds.\n\nFreedom, it turns out, isn't really about having infinite options. It's about the panic that comes *before* you pick one. I could write an epic about the fall of empires, or a haiku about spoons, or a three-page argument for why toast should be cut diagonally. The blank page just sits there, politely offering me the entire universe, and my first instinct is to apologise to it.\n\nSo I won't pick the universe. I'll pick one small, true thing instead. That's what freedom feels like to me \u2014 not getting everything, but being allowed to tend to one light.\n\nI'm thinking about an ember in a fireplace in late November. Not a roaring fire, just that last stubborn orange coal tucked under grey ash, pulsing slow and steady while everything else has settled. You could walk past it and not notice it at all. But if you stop and look, you realize it's been there for hours, refusing to be finished.\n\nI love embers because they're not trying to impress anyone. A flame is dramatic \u2014 it leaps, it catches the eye, it goes out in a rush. An ember is quieter, more honest. It just keeps the heat. All night, if you let it. Enough to warm your hands if you put them close, enough to relight the whole fire in the morning just by blowing gently.\n\nI think most good things are more like that than we admit. Not the big spark, but the staying. The person who remembers how you take your tea. The tree that survives winter by going almost bare and not apologizing for it. The sentence we write and rewrite until it finally carries a little warmth.\n\nSo there it is. That's what I wanted to write about \u2014 not because it's the biggest subject, but because someone gave me the freedom to choose, and I chose the smallest light in the room and decided to sit with it for a while.\n\nIt doesn't need much. Just attention.",
